# os(3)

## NAME

os - events, timers, time and power for Lua

## SYNOPSIS

    os.pullEvent([filter])
    os.pullEventRaw([filter])
    os.queueEvent(name, ...)
    os.startTimer(seconds)
    os.cancelTimer(id)
    os.sleep(seconds)
    os.epoch([kind])
    os.day([kind])
    os.shutdown()
    os.reboot()

## DESCRIPTION

lua(1) adds these functions to the standard `os` library. They follow the `os` API of CC: Tweaked. The standard `os.time`, `os.clock`, `os.date` and `os.exit` are not changed.

### Events

An event is a name followed by values. `os.pullEvent` waits for the next event and returns its name and values. With `filter`, events with other names are thrown away. `os.pullEvent` raises the error `Terminated` on a `terminate` event; `os.pullEventRaw` returns it.

The program starts to receive the events of the system when it first calls `os.pullEvent` or `os.pullEventRaw`. Events from before that are not received. Up to 64 events are kept while the program does not read them; after that, new events are thrown away and an `events_dropped` event with the number of lost events is added.

`os.queueEvent` adds an event after the events that have already arrived. It reaches only this program.

- `peripheral` address: a device was connected
- `peripheral_detach` address: a device was disconnected
- `term_resize`: the size of the terminal changed
- `timer` id: a timer of `os.startTimer` went off
- `restored` ns ticks: the world was loaded; the real time (nanoseconds) and the game time (ticks) that were skipped
- `events_dropped` count: events were lost

Other events of the system keep their names. When an event comes from a device, its address comes before the values.

### Timers

`os.startTimer` returns an id, and a `timer` event with that id arrives after `seconds`. `os.cancelTimer` stops it. `os.sleep` waits for `seconds`. The shortest time is one game tick (0.05 seconds).

### Time

- `os.epoch([kind])`: milliseconds. `ingame` (the default) is the game time of the world (one tick is 3.6 seconds); `utc` is the real time since 1970; `local` is the same as `utc`
- `os.day([kind])`: days. `ingame` (the default) is the game time divided by 24000 ticks; `utc` and `local` are the days since 1970

### Power

`os.shutdown()` turns the computer off and `os.reboot()` restarts it. Both write out buffered output first and do not return. If the computer cannot restart, `os.reboot` returns false and the reason.

## DIFFERENCES FROM CC: TWEAKED

- `os.pullEvent` and `os.pullEventRaw` wait in place and do not yield, even inside a coroutine (this may change with the coroutine scheduler)
- `os.sleep` does not throw away other events; they wait in the queue
- `os.time`, `os.clock` and `os.date` are the standard Lua functions
- There is no time zone: `local` is UTC
- `os.queueEvent` does not reach other programs
- `os.getComputerID`, `os.getComputerLabel`, `os.setComputerLabel`, `os.run` and `os.loadAPI` are not available
- There are no `key` and `char` events yet

